# Where DuckFleet deploys. The one place any of it is named.
#
# These are defaults, not constants: every one may be overridden from the
# environment, so pointing the lane at a DuckFleet-owned cluster is a change here
# rather than a change spread across scripts, a workflow and a chart.
#
# The values below describe the shared entity-resolution development cluster, which is
# where this deploys. DuckFleet does not own that cluster, and for a while that read as
# the problem: capacity there was not ours to grant, which blocked 2-CPU workers
# (entity-resolution-engine#1086) and pod-create RBAC for isolated compute (#39).
#
# Neither was really about cluster ownership. Both were things the *release* was trying
# to do that a release should not: grant its own permissions, and assume someone else's
# node pool would be sized for it. Both now live in infra/kubernetes/boundary, applied
# once by an operator with cluster admin — a grant and a node pool DuckFleet owns,
# inside a cluster it shares.
#
# What remains is genuinely shared and deliberately accepted: the control plane, the
# VPC, and the cluster's lifecycle. Their upgrades and outages are ours, and an
# administrator there can change our boundary objects. The taint and the quota bound
# DuckFleet's impact on its neighbours, not the reverse. infra/terraform/cluster is the
# exit if that trade stops being worth it; infra/README.md states what it costs.

# The EKS cluster, and the local kubeconfig context that must point at it. The
# context is asserted against the cluster's real endpoint before anything is applied,
# so a stale or wrong context fails rather than deploying somewhere unintended.
DUCKFLEET_CLUSTER=${DUCKFLEET_CLUSTER:-er-dev}
DUCKFLEET_KUBE_CONTEXT=${DUCKFLEET_KUBE_CONTEXT:-er-dev-admin}

# The namespace DuckFleet owns on that cluster. Unlike the rest, this is ours.
DUCKFLEET_NAMESPACE=${DUCKFLEET_NAMESPACE:-duckfleet-dev}

# The EKS NodeClass the DuckFleet node pool draws its node configuration from — the
# node IAM role, and the subnet and security group selectors. Referenced by name rather
# than defined, because those are facts about a cluster this repository does not own and
# would otherwise have to restate and keep in step.
#
# `default` is what EKS Auto Mode creates. A cluster whose built-in node pools are
# disabled may name it something else; scripts/aws/apply-boundary.sh lists what exists
# if this does not, rather than applying a node pool that can never provision.
DUCKFLEET_NODE_CLASS=${DUCKFLEET_NODE_CLASS:-default}

DUCKFLEET_REGION=${DUCKFLEET_REGION:-us-east-2}
DUCKFLEET_ACCOUNT=${DUCKFLEET_ACCOUNT:-797781631727}

# The CI deploy identity, which GitHub assumes over OIDC. bootstrap-state.sh grants it
# access to the state bucket through the bucket's own policy — same account, so a
# resource-policy allow suffices — rather than editing the role, which the shared
# platform owns. That is how the state dependency moved to a bucket DuckFleet owns
# without needing an entity-resolution change (#12).
DUCKFLEET_CI_ROLE=${DUCKFLEET_CI_ROLE:-duckfleet-ci-dev}

# The SSO profile an operator uses. Unset under GitHub Actions, where OIDC supplies
# short-lived environment credentials instead.
DUCKFLEET_AWS_PROFILE=${DUCKFLEET_AWS_PROFILE:-er-platform}

# Whether the namespace must carry the DuckFleet boundary label before a deploy runs.
#
# It should: the label is applied by scripts/aws/apply-boundary.sh along with the grant,
# the quota and the node pool, so its absence means the deploy is about to install a
# release into a namespace whose gateway cannot create the pods it advertises and whose
# workers have no pool sized for them. Failing first is the useful answer.
#
# This used to describe the label as a *shared-platform* bootstrap that only existed on
# a cluster DuckFleet did not own, with ownership implying nothing to wait for. That had
# it backwards: the boundary is what establishes ownership of a namespace, so it is
# needed wherever DuckFleet deploys. Overridable only so apply-boundary.sh, which is
# what satisfies the requirement, can run before it is satisfied.
DUCKFLEET_REQUIRE_BOOTSTRAP=${DUCKFLEET_REQUIRE_BOOTSTRAP:-true}

# Where Terraform keeps its state — a bucket DuckFleet owns. It used to be an
# entity-resolution bucket DuckFleet held only a key within; #12 moved the state out.
# bootstrap-state.sh created this bucket (versioned, encrypted, TLS-only, lifecycle),
# migrate-state.sh copied the nine tracked resources into it, and a plan against the
# new backend confirmed no changes before this default was flipped — so the move was
# complete before the lane was pointed here.
DUCKFLEET_STATE_BUCKET=${DUCKFLEET_STATE_BUCKET:-duckfleet-terraform-state-797781631727}
DUCKFLEET_STATE_KEY=${DUCKFLEET_STATE_KEY:-duckfleet/dev.tfstate}

# The cluster module keeps its own state: it is applied by an operator, not by CI, so
# a mistake there cannot touch the state CI writes.
DUCKFLEET_CLUSTER_STATE_KEY=${DUCKFLEET_CLUSTER_STATE_KEY:-duckfleet/cluster.tfstate}
